Bloom's floating tab bar and progressive blur
(src/tab-bar/, src/progressive-blur/)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Derived from expo-glass-tabs v0.1.1
  https://github.com/davidmokos/expo-glass-tabs
  MIT License, Copyright (c) 2026 David Mokos

Bloom derives the interaction and motion design from expo-glass-tabs v0.1.1: the
minimize-on-scroll progress model, the sliding highlight with finger scrubbing,
the constant-driven capsule geometry, the ten-layer progressive-blur falloff, and
the fade-on-focus tab screen. Each derived module carries a provenance header
naming the upstream file and version it came from.

The following are re-implemented rather than ported:

  - Theming. Upstream hardcodes a dark palette (#FFFFFF tints, an
    rgba(10,10,12,0.55) surface, a black blur tail), which reads as a black bar
    in a light theme. Every color role is instead resolved from Bloom's design
    tokens through `resolveTabBarTheme`, so the bar follows the active
    `BloomThemeProvider` preset and mode, with an optional partial override.

  - Icons. Upstream's icon API is SF Symbols. Bloom's primary API is
    `item.icon`, any node — the same convention as `Tabs`/`TabsTrigger` and
    `FrostedIconButton` — so the bar renders identically on every platform.
    `item.sfSymbol` remains available as an iOS-only enhancement.

  - Platform forks. Upstream is one iOS-first module. Bloom splits the capsule
    surface and the glyph into neutral / `.native` / `.web` variants, and forks
    the progressive blur so the web build uses one masked `backdrop-filter`
    instead of ten stacked `BlurView`s. The native-only packages therefore never
    reach a web bundle or a consumer's type-check.

  - Router coupling. Upstream binds directly to expo-router. Bloom's core
    `@oxyhq/bloom/tab-bar` has no router dependency at all; the bindings live in
    a separate `@oxyhq/bloom/tab-bar/expo-router` subpath, so the bar is usable
    in an app with no router.

  - Reanimated dependency arrays. Every mapper declares the shared values it
    reads, which is what makes the animation run on web without the
    react-native-worklets babel plugin.

