Behaviour derived from rn-primitives
  https://github.com/roninoss/rn-primitives
  MIT License, Copyright (c) 2024 roninoss

Public API derived from react-native-reusables, revision 119d0b10 (2026-07-02)
  https://github.com/founded-labs/react-native-reusables
  MIT License, Copyright (c) 2025 Founded Labs

The two are one layer each of the same stack, which is why they share an entry:
react-native-reusables is the STYLING layer — component names, composition and
prop shapes — and each of its families is a thin wrapper over the rn-primitives
package of the same name, which holds the BEHAVIOUR.

Bloom reads both as reference and ships neither as a dependency. What was
studied and re-expressed here is the native interaction model: open/close
semantics, the trigger/content/overlay split, per-row dismissal, the sub-menu
disclosure, the screen-reader focus handling, and the accessibility roles and
states each part carries.

WHAT IS RE-IMPLEMENTED RATHER THAN PORTED

  - The entire WEB half. Every `*.web.tsx` upstream is an adapter over Radix UI
    (`@radix-ui/react-dropdown-menu`, `-context-menu`, `-menubar`, `-select`,
    `-popover`, `-checkbox`); the behaviour on web is Radix's, not
    rn-primitives'. Bloom's web surfaces are its own — `floating/FloatingPanel`
    over `portal/`, `overlay/OverlayRoot` and `overlay/dropdown-placement`. No
    Radix code is included and Radix is not a dependency.

    The honest consequence: Bloom has no web arrow-key navigation, roving focus
    or type-ahead. Neither does rn-primitives — measured across its whole tree,
    type-ahead appears zero times and arrow keys four times, all in a package
    Bloom did not port and all either forwarded to Radix or discarded on native.
    Its only `onKeyDown` maps Enter and Space onto a press.

  - Portal and overlay host. Bloom uses `portal/` plus `overlay/OverlayRoot`,
    whose stack rank is taken on MOUNT by `overlay/stack.ts` so a surface opened
    later paints above one opened earlier. Upstream's portal host names and
    per-surface z-indices are not used; no Bloom overlay carries a `zIndex` of
    its own.

  - Positioning. `overlay/dropdown-placement.ts` (fit, flip, clamp, with
    `align` of start/center/end) rather than upstream's `useRelativePosition`.
    Its native anchored positioning has no caller here at all: a Bloom menu
    presents as a bottom sheet on native through `dialog/SheetShell`, so there
    is nothing to anchor.

  - `Slot` / `asChild`. Taken for TRIGGERS only, as `floating/TriggerSlot`, and
    deliberately declined for rows. A Bloom row IS `item/Item`, so `asChild`
    there would replace the component that guarantees the touch target, the
    disabled treatment, the destructive colour and the ARIA state — it would
    hand back exactly what the row exists to provide. Upstream can offer it
    because its rows are unstyled `Pressable`s that guarantee none of those.

  - The row vocabulary. Upstream ships three copy-pasted row sets, one per menu
    family, differing only in prefix. Bloom has ONE implementation, bound per
    family by `floating/menu-rows.tsx`'s `createMenuRows`, so the three families'
    rows cannot drift apart.

  - The hooks. Bloom already had equivalents of `useControllableState`,
    `useComposedRefs` (`mergeRefs`) and the interaction-state pairs, so only the
    two screen-reader focus hooks were genuinely absent, and those are rewritten
    rather than copied: the platform guard is read at the call site so the web
    branch is reachable by a test, the settle delay is a named constant with its
    reason, a pending focus move is cancelled if the surface closes inside the
    window, the restore fires on the open-to-closed EDGE only, and the
    `findNodeHandle` narrowing lives in one place instead of at each call.

  - `BackHandler`. Not taken. Upstream needs it because it portals into the app
    tree; every Bloom native surface is an RN `<Modal>`, which already fires
    `onRequestClose` on Android's back gesture. A second handler would race the
    first.

  - `textValue`. Re-expressed as `accessibilityLabel`, because in Bloom that is
    the whole of what it does — upstream's other use for it, matching keystrokes
    for type-ahead, has nothing to feed here.

  - `aspect-ratio`. Not ported in any sense. Upstream's is 22 lines whose body
    is one style property React Native has always had; Bloom's is its own, and
    differs deliberately by filling its parent's width and by refusing
    `className`.

  - `checkbox`. Bloom's predates this work. Only the web focus ring and a 44dp
    touch target were taken.

  - From react-native-reusables specifically: `cn`,
    `class-variance-authority`, `TextClassContext`, its `text.tsx`,
    `lucide-react-native`, `react-native-screens`' `FullWindowOverlay`, every
    hardcoded colour and every hardcoded `z-*`. Bloom resolves colour through
    `theme/accent-colors.ts` and design tokens, ships `icons/`, and wires
    `className` through `styled()` from react-native-css.

The sub-menu (`*MenuSub` / `*MenuSubTrigger` / `*MenuSubContent`) is Bloom's own
implementation on both platforms, and matches what upstream presents on each: a
FLYOUT panel beside the trigger row on web, an INLINE DISCLOSURE on native.
Upstream's web flyout is `@radix-ui/react-dropdown-menu`'s, so no code was taken
— the hover intent, the keyboard handling and the horizontal placement axis in
`floating/menu-sub-flyout.tsx` and `overlay/dropdown-placement.ts` are written
here against the same behaviour. Documented in `docs/dropdown-menu.mdx`.

Every module named above carries Bloom's own file header and no per-file
provenance comment, because none of them contains upstream code.

